WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.048
The stage you didn’t build last lesson.

2
00:00:02.328 --> 00:00:08.707
Your Shakespeare GPT read characters: a vocabulary of a few dozen symbols, one per character.

3
00:00:08.987 --> 00:00:10.736
Production language models don’t.

4
00:00:11.016 --> 00:00:22.792
They read tokens from a vocabulary of tens or hundreds of thousands of chunks, produced by a separate component, the tokenizer, which has its own training data and its own training algorithm.

5
00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:34.635
The lecture’s description stresses that it is a completely separate stage of the pipeline, with exactly two jobs after training: encode strings to tokens, and decode tokens back to strings.

6
00:00:34.915 --> 00:00:40.973
This lecture is about that stage, and it’s the most directly useful one in the series for an applied engineer.

7
00:00:41.253 --> 00:00:47.397
Token counts are what you pay for, what context windows are measured in, and what latency scales with.

8
00:00:47.677 --> 00:00:56.104
And, as the lecture shows in its last half hour, a surprising number of odd model behaviors trace back to how text was cut into tokens.

9
00:00:56.384 --> 00:01:00.032
The lecture builds the GPT-2 tokenizer from scratch.

10
00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:06.264
Its companion repository, minbpe (MIT), contains the finished code, and its exercise.

11
00:01:06.544 --> 00:01:11.557
md lays out the build in stages so you can try each one before watching the answer.
